<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/8th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Fakhkh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/786">786</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Fakhkh was fought in June 786 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, where Abbasid forces crushed a revolt of the Alid al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a>, a descendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-ali">Hasan ibn Ali</a>. Al-Husayn and many of his followers were killed. Idris ibn Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a>, a survivor, fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, where he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alids">Alids</a>, chiefly of the Zaydi and Hasanid branches, rejected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> as usurpers, and al-Mansur had faced the revolt of Muhammad al-Nafs al-Zakiyya in 762 to 763. Al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a> was a close relative of that rebel: his father Ali al-Abid, famed for his piety, died in al-Mansur's prisons in 763. The historian Laura Veccia Vaglieri describes Husayn's upbringing as an atmosphere of extreme piety and secret hatred for the Abbasids, though he was on friendly terms with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>.</p><p>After al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> died in July 785, the Abbasid governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-umari">al-Umari</a>, had three men, among them Abu'l-Zift, a son of Muhammad al-Nafs al-Zakiyya, flogged for drinking alcohol, and quarrelled with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alids">Alids</a> when Abu'l-Zift fled. The revolt had been planned for some time, with Kufans waiting secretly in the city and hopes of help from pilgrims to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">Hajj</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On the morning of 16 May 786 about 26 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alids">Alids</a> and their supporters gathered at the Prophet's Mosque, where Husayn, dressed in white, received oaths as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> under the title al-Murtada. Musa ibn Ja'far al-Kazim, the seventh imam of the Twelver Shi'a, refused his support. The rebels drove off about 200 men of the garrison, but, in al-Tabari's words, the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> locked their doors. Penned into the mosque, Husayn left the city with about 300 men on 28 May.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> al-Hadi entrusted the response to Muhammad ibn Sulayman ibn Ali, who gathered the armed retinues of the Abbasid princes returning from the pilgrimage. On 11 June the armies met in the wadi of Fakhkh, about 4 kilometres north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. After Muhammad ibn Sulayman won on his wing he led the right and centre against the massed Alid army, which was routed. Husayn refused an offer of clemency and fought until he was killed; his head was sent to al-Hadi, who sent it on to Khurasan as a warning.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Over a hundred rebels fell and lay unburied for three days. Abu'l-Zift surrendered but was killed, and several Alid prisoners were executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. Yahya ibn Abdallah and his brother Idris escaped among the pilgrims; Idris founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty</a> in 789, and Yahya rose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a> in 792. The wadi became known as al-Shuhada, the Martyrs, second only to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karbala">Karbala</a> in Shi'a martyrology for the number of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alids">Alids</a> killed. Husayn's oath, modelled on that of Zayd ibn Ali in 740, later shaped Zaydi practice, and the Alids who scattered carried their loyalties to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Fakhkh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the <b>Battle of Fakhkh</b>, near Mecca, between the Abbasids and the supporters of Muhammad's descendants, he fled west, reached Tangier and by 788 had settled at Volubilis, Walili in Arabic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
